# Question: is it worth automating a routine task (the "Is It Worth the
# Time?" table from xkcd 1205, with ranges), and how likely is the time it
# saves to exceed the time spent building and maintaining it?
#
# Run it:
#   ubc --scenario engineering/automation
# Change an assumption, for example a task done only once or twice a week:
#   ubc --set 'runs_per_week = 1 to 2' --scenario engineering/automation
# See which assumption matters most:
#   ubc --sens net_hours --scenario engineering/automation
#
# Every "a to b" is a 90% confidence interval. Everything is in hours of the
# same people, so the cost of an hour cancels out. Like the xkcd table, it
# looks five years ahead; unlike it, it counts keeping the automation working.
#
# Sources: Randall Munroe, xkcd 1205 "Is It Worth the Time?", for the
# automation table and its five-year horizon; the ranges are typical
# experience.

# Assumptions
minutes_saved = 2 to 10           # minutes the task takes by hand each time; time it yourself
runs_per_week = 2 to 8            # times a week someone does it; count them in your logs or tracker
weeks_per_year = 44               # working weeks, minus holidays; rule of thumb
years = 5                         # horizon, as in the xkcd 1205 table
build_hours = 16 to 60            # hours to write and test the automation; typical experience
maintenance_hours = 4 to 16       # hours a year keeping it working as things around it change; typical experience

# Calculation
hours_saved = minutes_saved * runs_per_week * weeks_per_year * years / 60   # time no longer spent by hand
hours_spent = build_hours + maintenance_hours * years                       # time spent on the automation
net_hours = hours_saved - hours_spent                                       # positive means it was worth it

# Result
hours_saved
hours_spent
P(net_hours > 0)                  # how likely is automating it to save more time than it takes?
